
For my Checkoff 3 project, I chose to explore reference counting in Python, specifically how CPython keeps track of objects in memory and determines when they can be deallocated. 
I chose this topic because I wanted to understand more about how programming languages manage memory behind the scenes. 
Since COMP 211 has introduced concepts involving memory, pointers, and programming in C, I was interested in comparing how memory management works in C and Python, since I primarily program in Python.

I initially used AI to get a general understanding of reference counting and how Python uses it. I learned that variables in Python refer to objects in memory, and multiple variables can reference the same object. 
CPython keeps track of these references using reference counts. When an object's reference count reaches zero, CPython can deallocate that object. 
As I explored the topic further, I became interested in what happens when variables are deleted, how reference counts change, and why circular references can cause problems.

Some of the prompts I used to guide my exploration included asking AI to explain how reference counting works, requesting code examples that demonstrate changes in reference counts, and investigating how Python handles circular references. 
These questions helped me move beyond simply learning definitions and understand how the concepts work in actual programs.

I used AI as a learning tool by asking follow-up questions whenever an explanation was unclear and requesting simpler examples to make abstract concepts easier to understand. 
I also connected the topic to concepts from COMP 211, especially pointers and memory management in C. One particularly interesting discovery was that reference counting alone cannot always reclaim objects involved in circular references. 
I learned that CPython uses a cyclic garbage collector to help identify and reclaim unreachable reference cycles. This helped me understand that Python's memory management involves more than just tracking individual reference counts.

Overall, using AI helped me break a complicated topic into smaller concepts and explore questions I might not have thought to investigate on my own. 
